+# 1. Purpose
+
+This dictionary defines component-normative vocabulary for Document Identity within explicitly qualified subjects, domains and responsible contracts. The definitions contained herein are normative for the Document Identity component in that declared scope; this is not a claim of universal authority over every framework domain or of an approved implementation.
+
+Common Architecture/Policy and responsible identity, descriptor, category, connection, registration, projection and lifecycle sources retain their applicable authority. DF-ARC-006 supplies owner-linked common consultation vocabulary and contextual aliases rather than replacing every detailed domain contract. Explicit applicable declarations, not Type Reference, a title or a repeated definition, establish normative authority.
+
+The dictionary covers applicable assigned normalized logical documents and their identity-related meanings, while distinguishing pre-allocation subjects, files, exact editions/Status, mutable records and other entity domains. Every README.md, CHANGELOG.md and ArchitecturalMilestones.md remains non-normalized at every location. No identity, Metadata field/Profile, permission or operational service is created by naming a term.
+
+---
+
+# 2. Introduction
+
+Interpret terms with their stated subject/domain/context, responsible source editions and controlled alias limits. Generic words such as Identity, Identifier, Lifecycle and Validation also have other entity-domain uses; documentary context does not erase those contracts.
+
+Where common terminology and a component source appear to conflict, identify the actual responsible meaning, previous/current editions and affected consumers under common governing contracts. Do not declare either glossary universally superior, silently select one claim or infer that all sources have been reconciled. Preserve unresolved authority and narrower unambiguous observations; coordinated owner decisions and propagation remain necessary before broader conformity claims.
+
+Canonical semantic meaning is distinct from document lifecycle Status, formal approval, publication or exact Baseline inclusion. A dictionary's presence, a working Draft source or a local correction PASS grants none of those outcomes. Prior semantic/attribution-only results retain their exact bytes, editions and scope.
+
+---
+
+# 3. Dictionary
+
+The thirty received term labels remain in their original order:twenty-nine numbered entries plus unnumbered Non-Reusability between3.23 and3.24. The unnumbered heading is intentionally retained; it is not a new allocation or a reason to shift later anchors.
+
+Each definition and its qualified Notes identify a subject, responsible meaning and evidence boundary. Definition/Notes organization is preserved and supplemented explicitly; no missing historical rule or event is invented. Current source roles are linked in §8, with exact comparison paths/editions/status/hashes retained externally.
+
+---
+
+## 3.1. Assignment
+
+**Definition**
+
+In this documentary context, the governed act or event establishing an assigned qualified document key and its association with the intended applicable logical document under the responsible domain/allocation contract.
+
+**Notes**
+
+Assignment is a contextual short form of Identity Assignment (§3.11). One governing association is not recreated for every copy, content revision, registration or descriptor record. Pre-allocation and functional-file exclusions still apply.
+
+The received Notes—occurs exactly once, creates the canonical identity, and does not imply approval or publication—are qualified as an actual responsible assignment decision/event, not automatic allocation from recognition or file creation. Actual authority/rules, subject/domain, collision/reservation/history and resulting association evidence are needed. No algorithm, user role or permission is supplied; assignment alone selects no Baseline member.
+
+---
+
+## 3.2. Canonical Identifier
+
+**Definition**
+
+The canonical identifier representation or interpretation used for an assigned identity under its governing domain contract; in documentary scope, a contextual role of Identifier (§3.21), not a second logical identity.
+
+**Notes**
+
+The four received qualities remain scoped:
+
+| Quality | Qualified meaning / evidence boundary |
+|---|---|
+| Unique | Actual full qualified assignment/canonicalization within the governing domain and declared population/history |
+| Permanent | Preserve same-document association, reservations and historical interpretation |
+| Immutable | Assigned key continuity through ordinary evolution, not every supporting record |
+| Referenceable | Intended qualified target/exact-edition interpretation; actual resolution and live availability require observations |
+
+Canonical does not mean approved/published document Status or Baseline inclusion. Legitimate aliases/renderings require responsible mappings and do not alone allocate another key. See Domain (§3.12), Uniqueness (§3.28) and the corpus contract in §3.21.
+
+---
+
+## 3.3. Canonical Identity
+
+**Definition**
+
+The governing assigned document identity in its declared domain/canonical interpretation, contextually equivalent to Document Identity for the intended applicable assigned normalized logical document.
+
+**Notes**
+
+The received every-document/exactly-one statement applies to the governing document/domain assignment subject, not every physical file, pre-allocation subject, exact edition, category or other entity. Copies/aliases/editions are not automatically different logical identities. Canonical naming does not imply document Status=Canonical/Approved, publication or Baseline membership.
+
+---
+
+## 3.4. Document Identity
+
+**Definition**
+
+The enduring logical document identity under its applicable qualified Domain+Identifier allocation/preservation contract, associated with the same assigned normalized logical document through ordinary evolution.
+
+**Notes**
+
+The six received independence subjects remain:
+
+- metadata;
+- document content;
+- repository;
+- implementation;
+- storage;
+- governance.
+
+These are semantic separation boundaries, not absence of actual contracts, decisions or evidence. Supporting descriptors/state/reference/index/projection records may change while the assigned key remains; logical representations/interfaces and actual required provider contracts still matter. Physical file, exact edition/Status and Baseline member are different subjects. Necessary governing-contract/representation transition follows §9; no arbitrary reassignment, mutation or global independence guarantee is introduced.
+
+---
+
+## 3.5. Document Identity Component
+
+**Definition**
+
+The registered Documentation component that defines document-key/domain meaning and applicable allocation, preservation, lifecycle, validation and identity-governance contracts under common Architecture/Policy.
+
+**Notes**
+
+Contract responsibility is distinct from actual assignment, checking, retirement or management execution. Applicable DMS, tool or manual processes execute real operations, with CMS governing changes in its scope. Actual authority/decision, exact subject/rules/editions/context, executor and observations require evidence. Naming the component does not install an engine, allocate a key or grant a permission; other semantic/record owners retain their contracts.
+
+---
+
+## 3.6. Document Identity Lifecycle
+
+**Definition**
+
+The subject-specific identity assertion/evolution contract covering an applicable documentary pre-allocation subject and, after assignment, continuing same-document preservation and actual retirement of active use within a declared scope.
+
+**Notes**
+
+DID-006 distinguishes Unassigned pre-allocation from Assigned, Preserved and Retired assertions; a nonexistent assigned key is not presumed to occupy Unassigned. Its eight selected transition rows require actual events, responsible decision/rules and evidence, not a universal permission graph. Identity Lifecycle (§3.15) is a documentary-context alias with the same scope.
+
+Document-edition Status, identity-state records, maturity, approval, publication and Baseline membership differ. Status, archiving or deletion of a file/edition/index alone does not retire the logical identity or grant an unlisted transition. Actual State/Canonical Reference and complete-record/lifecycle authority in DID-005/006/014 remains pending reconciliation, not waived or merged here.
+
+---
+
+## 3.7. Document Identity Model
+
+**Definition**
+
+The responsible conceptual contract or selected view of documentary identity subjects, elements, associations and ownership boundaries in its explicitly declared scope.
+
+**Notes**
+
+A conceptual model is not automatically a complete accepted implementation record or object layout. DID-003 and detailed owners retain entity/cardinality meaning; DID-005 selected key/association view differs from actual mandatory State/Canonical Reference/lifecycle claims in DID-014. Preserve those requirements and pending authority rather than invent a reconciled merged model or infer completeness from a picture or glossary.
+
+---
+
+## 3.8. Document Identity Schema
+
+**Definition**
+
+The responsible structural contract for the declared identity/record/association subjects, representation constraints and applicable cardinalities, as qualified by its source editions and owner rules.
+
+**Notes**
+
+DID-005 is the schema source; its selected assigned key/association scope does not remove DID-014 mandatory State/Canonical Reference or settle lifecycle authority. Canonical structure is not an approved universal record merely because a definition calls it canonical. Necessary schema/domain/representation evolution requires §9 prior/new authority, owner/consumer compatibility and transition evidence; no field, Metadata Element/Profile or accepted complete schema is fabricated.
+
+---
+
+## 3.9. Document Reference
+
+**Definition**
+
+A document-targeting reference identifying its intended existing qualified logical document or exact edition with the additional coordinates/bytes and context required by its responsible endpoint/connection contract.
+
+**Notes**
+
+In this documentary target scope, Document Reference is a controlled alias of Identity Reference (§3.17); other identity endpoint kinds retain their own contracts. An intended target is distinct from successful performed resolution, complete connection validity or live retrieval. Incoming references may be0..N; that does not waive validity of each actual reference. Preserve alias/source/edition/context/provenance and actual dangling, ambiguous, stale or unavailable observations; never silently create a key or retarget immutable historical members.
+
+---
+
+## 3.10. Identity
+
+**Definition**
+
+In a qualified entity-domain context, the persistent characteristic or key by which the intended entity is recognized under its responsible contract; in this component, documentary use refers to Document Identity (§3.4) unless another endpoint/domain is explicitly identified.
+
+**Notes**
+
+This contextual use preserves ARC-006§5.17 generic entity-domain meaning. It does not redefine every identity as a document identity, allocate DF IDs to every entity or equate the logical subject with its identifier string. Governing Domain+Identifier, rendering, mutable properties, file and exact edition are distinct.
+
+---
+
+## 3.11. Identity Assignment
+
+**Definition**
+
+The actual governed association event by which an applicable intended logical document receives its assigned qualified key under the responsible allocation/decision contract.
+
+**Notes**
+
+Assignment (§3.1) is the contextual short form; Identity Creation in §4 is an event alias only in this evidenced allocation scope. Recognition, generating a string/header, file creation, a provisional tracking label or registration alone is not allocation. Record actual domain/series authority, rules/source editions, collision/reservation/history and resulting association. Subsequent ordinary evolution preserves the key; no new user role, state, permission or missing historical decision is invented.
+
+---
+
+## 3.12. Identity Domain
+
+**Definition**
+
+The governing semantic scope and responsible contract within which assigned keys are qualified and allocation, uniqueness, canonicalization, preservation and reservation rules apply.
+
+**Notes**
+
+Different explicit domain contracts may coexist; qualification and actual assignments/canonical interpretations determine the claim, not a universal uniqueness guarantee from a domain label. Documentation Framework is the adopted documentary domain, represented by DF; external entities/domains retain their own contracts. One governing assigned document/domain association is preserved through ordinary evolution. Domain prefix/rendering is distinct from the semantic subject, mutable Family/Type and current repository. Necessary domain-contract transition requires §9 assessment, not silent reassignment.
+
+---
+
+## 3.13. Identity Governance
+
+**Definition**
+
+The identity-domain requirements and responsibility/evidence boundaries governing assigned same-document key integrity, stability, preservation, reservations and responsible change under applicable contracts.
+
+**Notes**
+
+DID-008 owns detailed identity-governance requirements; common architecture and other semantic/record owners remain applicable. Governance is the area grouping Workflow, Policies, Reviews and Baselines documentary contracts. Actual DMS/tool/manual execution and CMS change scope are distinct and need real authority/decision/executor evidence. Documentary ownership, authorship or a local PASS is not operational permission, formal approval, publication or exact Baseline selection.
+
+---
+
+## 3.14. Identity Integrity
+
+**Definition**
+
+A scoped requirement or assessed property that the intended document-key association and applicable supporting facts satisfy their responsible completeness, provenance/authenticity and consistency rules in an identified subject/context.
+
+**Notes**
+
+The received complete/authentic/internally-consistent qualities require actual applicable rules/editions, artifacts, authority, observations, coverage and limits; they are not an unconditional implemented guarantee. No universal authentication, cryptographic assurance or security mechanism is supplied by this definition.
+
+Completeness depends on actual mandatory owner fields/records, including unresolved State/Canonical Reference/lifecycle authority. Missing evidence or conflicting authority withholds broader conformity without inventing a violation or an accepted merged schema. Integrity is distinct from document Status, approval, publication and Baseline membership.
+
+---
+
+## 3.15. Identity Lifecycle
+
+**Definition**
+
+Within this component, a contextual short form of Document Identity Lifecycle (§3.6): the responsible subject-specific pre-allocation, assignment, preservation and active-use retirement assertion/evolution contract.
+
+**Notes**
+
+The received assignment-until-retirement description concerns the assigned-key portion; it does not erase DID-006 Unassigned pre-allocation or imply a key already exists there. Selected eight transitions and actual event/decision coverage remain with DID-006. No universal state/permission graph, automatic document-status correlation or implicit additional edge is created. Other entity and document-edition lifecycles retain their owners.
+
+---
+
+## 3.16. Identity Preservation
+
+**Definition**
+
+The requirement and evidenced continuity of an assigned qualified key with the same logical document under its governing preservation contract through ordinary document evolution.
+
+**Notes**
+
+Preservation is not a guarantee that every supporting state/reference/descriptor/index/projection record remains unchanged or that live content is always retrievable. Actual sources/editions/context, observed changes and coverage substantiate a claim. Necessary normative/domain/representation transition requires §9 prior/new reconciliation, mappings/reservations/history and reference/exact-snapshot continuity; no reassignment to another document or silent historical retargeting is authorized.
+
+---
+
+## 3.17. Identity Reference
+
+**Definition**
+
+In this documentary component context, a reference to the intended existing qualified document identity or required exact edition/context under the responsible endpoint/connection contract.
+
+**Notes**
+
+Document Reference (§3.9) is a controlled alias only for this document-targeting scope. Other identity-reference endpoint kinds use their own domains and are not automatically DF document references. Relationships owns connection meaning/records; Registry registrations and Knowledge Graph projections retain their owners. Optional incoming0..N, actual target/alias validity, connection correctness, historical interpretation and live retrieval are distinct; intended reference alone is not a successful resolution outcome.
+
+---
+
+## 3.18. Identity Retirement
+
+**Definition**
+
+The actual responsible decision/event ending active use of an assigned document identity within its declared contract scope while retaining the same-document historical association and allocated-key reservation.
+
+**Notes**
+
+Retirement never permits identity reuse. Allocated as well as retired keys remain reserved against assignment to another logical document. Actual subject, decision/authority, rules/editions, event and reservation/history coverage are required; missing live content/index entries or a document deletion/status alone is not retirement or availability. Distinguish the event from the Retired assertion/state-oriented use in §3.26. No identity retirement is executed by this dictionary.
+
+---
+
+## 3.19. Identity Stability
+
+**Definition**
+
+The requirement and assessed property that assigned qualified key interpretation and same-document association remain stable during ordinary evolution under the governing contract.
+
+**Notes**
+
+Stability differs from freezing mutable records or all future normative definitions. Current Family/Type/Component/title/path/format/edition/Status changes do not reconstruct the stable allocated key. Necessary governing-contract change follows §9 with actual previous/new authority, affected owners/consumers, compatibility/version and continuity checks. No universal harmless-change, interoperability or availability guarantee follows.
+
+---
+
+## 3.20. Identity Validation
+
+**Definition**
+
+The scoped evaluation of applicable responsible identity requirements against exact identified subjects, artifacts/editions and context, with performed checks, observations, coverage and limits.
+
+**Notes**
+
+DID-007 defines Valid, Invalid, Inconsistent and Unresolvable observations. An arbitrary successful subset is narrower evidence, not full identity/component/framework Valid; missing/unperformed/conflicting mandatory evidence withholds broader success without automatic fabricated failure. Preserve actual negative observations and unresolved authority.
+
+Checking is distinct from writing a result record and from separately authorized correction/allocation. A local result does not mutate the assessed key, grant permission, formally approve/publish a document or select an exact Baseline member. Validation (§3.29) is a contextual short form within this component, not ownership of every framework check.
+
+---
+
+## 3.21. Identifier
+
+**Definition**
+
+The canonical representation or value used to reference the intended identity within its declared governing domain; for document identity, interpreted together with that domain rather than as the logical document itself.
+
+**Notes**
+
+Canonical Identifier (§3.2) is the contextual canonical role, not another key. For this corpus the domain is Documentation Framework, represented by DF. DF-SSS-NNN uses a registered uppercase three-letter stable allocation series and positive three-digit001–999 number. 000 is not allocated; gaps prove no availability; allocated/retired numbers are not reused. At999, register a traceable unused continuation series rather than widen syntax or recycle. Current Family/Type/Component/path/edition/Status does not reconstruct an assigned identifier; a recorded historical series purpose does not mandate renumbering. Other domains require explicit contracts.
+
+Actual canonical/alias interpretation, assignment/population/collision/reservation/history and coverage substantiate uniqueness. A bare string or matching syntax is not a global identity/resolver guarantee. Legitimate aliases/renderings and actual interfaces require responsible mappings; this source registers no series, alias or allocation.
+
+---
+
+## 3.22. Identifier Stability
+
+**Definition**
+
+The requirement and assessed continuity of an assigned canonical identifier interpretation during ordinary document evolution under its governing domain contract.
+
+**Notes**
+
+It is not a guarantee of unchanged every-record bytes or harmless future consumer behavior. Support records/aliases/locators/editions have their own rules. Necessary domain/representation/contract revision requires §9 prior/new authority, owner/consumer/interface compatibility, mappings/reservations/history and intended reference/exact-snapshot continuity before coordinated application; no arbitrary key replacement or reuse is allowed.
+
+---
+
+## 3.23. Immutability
+
+**Definition**
+
+In assigned document-key scope, the constraint preserving the governing Domain+Identifier key and its same-document association through ordinary evolution under the applicable contract.
+
+**Notes**
+
+Immutability does not mean every state/reference/descriptor/registration/projection record is frozen. Necessary assessed normative/representation revision follows §9 with explicit prior/new responsible authority and continuity evidence; that is not blanket permission to mutate a key, reassign it to another document or retarget historical members. Common architectural definitions can evolve under their owners; actual restrictive DID-014 extension claims remain pending reconciliation, not silently waived.
+
+---
+
+## Non-Reusability
+
+**Definition**
+
+The constraint that an allocated document key, including a retired key, is not reassigned to a different logical document under its governing allocation/reservation contract.
+
+**Notes**
+
+Preserve same-document association, actual reservation/retirement history and intended historical interpretation. A gap, missing record or unavailable content does not establish availability or an observed reassignment. The received unnumbered heading remains between3.23 and3.24; its external evidence key is not a new historic number, concept ID or document allocation.
+
+---
+
+## 3.24. Permanence
+
+**Definition**
+
+The requirement that assigned document identity remains associated with the same logical document, with historical recognition and reserved allocation retained under its governing contract even after active use ends.
+
+**Notes**
+
+Permanence is not perpetual live retrieval, validity of every connection or immutability of all support records. Actual preservation, retirement, alias/reference context and history evidence determine the scoped observation. It does not authorize key reuse or imply an approved exact edition/Baseline member.
+
+---
+
+## 3.25. Reference Resolution
+
+**Definition**
+
+An actual interpretation/check process seeking the intended qualified document target or exact edition/context of a document-targeting reference under its responsible contract.
+
+**Notes**
+
+Intended key interpretation, connection validity, performed resolution observations and locating/retrieving current content are different facts. Record exact target/aliases, rules/source editions, method/executor, context, observations, coverage and limits. Dangling, ambiguous, stale and unavailable evidence remains explicit; missing/unperformed evidence is not automatically an Unresolvable outcome. No running universal resolver, perpetual availability or silent key/target creation is defined here.
+
+---
+
+## 3.26. Retirement
+
+**Definition**
+
+In state-oriented use within the selected DID-006 contract, the Retired assertion following an actual responsible retirement event: active use ends in its declared scope while the same-document historical association and key reservation remain.
+
+**Notes**
+
+Distinguish this assertion from Identity Retirement event/decision (§3.18). DID-006 has no outgoing edge from Retired in its selected eight-transition scope; this does not establish every external lifecycle contract or a universal permission graph. Document archiving, obsolescence or file/edition/index deletion is not automatically retirement; history and reservations remain even if current content is unavailable. The received no-longer-active-document phrase does not erase original document association or permit reuse.
+
+---
+
+## 3.27. Traceability
+
+**Definition**
+
+The capability and evidence needed to follow the intended document/key association, exact editions, references, changes and responsible decisions through identified current/historical contexts.
+
+**Notes**
+
+Actual assignment, preservation/retirement, source/rule editions, mappings/aliases, contributor/decision provenance, observations and coverage support a scoped trace. Long-term understandable recognition is an objective/obligation, not proof that all history is complete, every future reference stays valid or content is permanently retrievable. Exact edition and snapshot selection require their additional evidence; logical identity alone is insufficient.
+
+---
+
+## 3.28. Uniqueness
+
+**Definition**
+
+The requirement and assessed property that a full qualified key/canonical interpretation does not have conflicting assignments to different logical documents within its governing domain/allocation contract.
+
+**Notes**
+
+Actual domain authority, assignments/subjects, canonicalization/aliases, population and current/historical collision/reservation coverage establish the claim. Copies, repeated registrations/projections, legitimate aliases and exact editions of one document are not alone duplicate allocations; equal suffixes across different registered series do not alone collide. Syntax, a domain label or partial index proves no global uniqueness; preserve missing-evidence limits rather than invent collisions or availability.
+
+---
+
+## 3.29. Validation
+
+**Definition**
+
+Within this component, the contextual short form of Identity Validation (§3.20): actual scoped evaluation against applicable responsible mandatory identity rules, not confirmation of every rule in the entire Documentation Framework.
+
+**Notes**
+
+Use DID-007 outcome/coverage meanings and exact subject/domain/rules/artifacts/edition/context, executor, checks and observations. Other check domains retain their owners. Missing/conflicting mandatory authority is not full success or a fabricated violation; preserved negative observations, result recording and separately authorized correction remain distinct. No mutation, approval, publication, Baseline selection or complete implemented conformity follows from the term.
